Because React compares props with `Object.is` (reference equality), not by deep value. An object literal creates a brand-new object every time the parent renders:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  return <Child style={{ color: "red" }} />;
  // each render: { color: "red" } is a NEW object with a new reference
}
```

`{ color: "red" } === { color: "red" }` is `false`, so from React's point of view the prop changed on every render.

There's also a more basic point: **a child re-renders whenever its parent re-renders, regardless of props.** Props only matter if you've opted out of that with `React.memo` (or `PureComponent` / `shouldComponentUpdate`). So the object literal is only a problem if the child is memoized. With `memo`, an unstable reference defeats it.

## Fixes

**1. Hoist constant objects out of the component**
```jsx
const style = { color: "red" };

function Parent() {
  return <Child style={style} />;
}
```

**2. `useMemo` when the object depends on props/state**
```jsx
function Parent({ color }) {
  const style = useMemo(() => ({ color }), [color]);
  return <Child style={style} />;
}
```

**3. Pass primitives instead of an object**
```jsx
<Child color="red" />
```
This is often the simplest option, since primitives compare by value.

**4. Wrap the child in `React.memo`** (needed alongside 1–3 for them to have any effect)
```jsx
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ style }) { ... });
```

**5. Custom comparator (last resort)**
```jsx
const Child = React.memo(Child, (prev, next) => shallowOrDeepEqual(prev.style, next.style));
```
Deep comparisons cost time on every render, so use this sparingly.

## Same issue applies to
- Arrays: `items={[1, 2, 3]}`
- Functions: `onClick={() => ...}` (use `useCallback` to stabilize)
- Objects in context values: `<Ctx.Provider value={{ a, b }}>` re-renders all consumers on every render. Memoize the value.

## Notes
- Don't memoize everything. Re-renders are usually cheap, and `useMemo`/`memo` have their own cost. Optimize when you've measured a problem (React DevTools Profiler).
- If you use the React Compiler (React 19+ ecosystem), it can auto-memoize many of these cases, so manual `useMemo`/`useCallback` is often unnecessary.

